look at the situation in india it is  absolutely  dire what more can we do well it's a  heartbreaking situation in india and my  heart goes out to the people of india  and the  severe problems they're facing the  united kingdom has already sent 600  pieces of equipment out  we're sending oxygen out and we've got  another shipment going out this week as  well  and we're working very very closely with  partners across the world  to make sure india has the supplies it  needs  and of course india was of huge help to  the united kingdom last year  making sure we had the paracetamol we  need they're a close  ally of the united kingdom and we do  really are working hard  to make sure that we can help as much as  possible i mean christian aid have said  now that you know most british people in  high-risk categories have been  vaccinated  we now need to start sharing our vaccine  supplies  with nations like india urgently well  we're already part of the kovacs  programme which is about supplying  vaccines around the world with the third  largest donor  of course the astrozenica vaccine is  being produced in  india and that has been done under  voluntary licence and i'm working very  closely with dr negozi  at the world trade organization to see  what more we can do to ramp up  vaccine supply across the world because  you're absolutely right  nobody is safe until everybody is safe  and we need to make sure that we  increase supply as much as possible of  course to continue with our vaccination  program here in the uk  but also to make sure the people of  india are vaccinated and across the  world but in terms of india  the major issue is oxygen that is our  focus that is the  uh product that we're getting out to  india as rapidly as possible because  that is the immediate issue they're  facing  but i have a call later today with my  indian counterpart  we're talking about the free trade  agreement negotiations but we're also  talking about  what more we can do to help this  immediate crisis in india  in in terms of the trade deal which has  been announced today i mean it is  significant  you're looking at a a billion pounds of  new uk  india trade and investment that is being  announced today  that is hugely significant but why is it  not a free trade deal  so first of all you know this is a  significant announcement it's going to  mean 6 000 new jobs  in the united kingdom uh including  areas like vaccinations so we've got the  serum institute of india  investing more in the united kingdom  there are also going to be more  opportunities  in india because this is about a win-win  for both countries it's about helping  india with the immediate cavite crisis  but also helping india build back  better after the covered crisis we will  start negotiations on a full free trade  agreement this autumn  of course free trade agreements take  longer what this is is this is the  immediate gains that we can get  for both countries uh driving jobs and  growth here in  britain and in india how quickly do you  think that you can work with with this  in place  how quickly can you then work towards a  free trade deal  we want to launch negotiations in autumn  we want to get those negotiations  completed as soon as possible but of  course  we'll be looking for early wins that we  can gain for both countries so for  example there are currently very high  tariffs on cars into india  on products like whiskey into india we  want to see those tariffs lowered  or removed to benefit industries here in  britain  and similarly there are products from  india that we want to see  here in the united kingdom and if you  think about it india is the world's  largest democracy  it's a rapidly uh growing economy  currently our trade is 24 billion we  think we can double that  by by 2030. and this is where britain  needs to be we need to be trading  with those fast-growing parts of the  world to the benefit of british people  right across the country  um can i ask you about the allegations  still circling around number 10 because  obviously we're we're now in an election  week got the local elections  on thursday how damaging do you think  these allegations are  in in the run-up to a local election  well i've been out campaigning  uh in the elections across the country  and what people want to know  is they want to know what are my  opportunities going to be  how fast is the government getting on  with the vaccination programme we just  had the announcement yesterday  we've done 50 million vaccines across  the uk  they want to know that we are on track  with our roadmap for  getting out of the covid lockdown that's  what people are concerned about and i  think these  allegations and counter rumors from  what's going on in number 10 are very  very little interest to people across  the country what they want to know is  how we're dealing with covid  how the vaccination program is going and  how we're building jobs and growth  today we're announcing these 6 000 more  jobs thanks to our trade  agreement with india there are reports  though in some of the papers this  morning  the times particularly saying there are  ministers  some of your cabinet colleagues who are  concerned about how this  is going to play out this week are you  one of those ministers  i'm a hundred percent focused on my job  of getting new trade deals for the  united kingdom  bringing jobs and growth across the  united kingdom getting more of our  fantastic  companies exporting their cars their  food and drink products  their it products around the world  that's my focus and that's what my  cabinet colleagues are focused on  and that's what the prime minister is  focused on he spends every waking hour  thinking about how we're dealing with  covid our vaccination program  and this is frankly a distraction from  those really important issues that  people care about  without looking at the allegations  particularly because  we know they've been you know the labour  party can say one thing  we're hearing another thing from downing  street they are being denied there are  investigations underway we get that  i'm not asking you to comment on whether  you think the prime minister has done  anything wrong he's denied it  but the fact that douglas ross the the  leader of this of the scottish  conservative party said  if codes had been broken  then the prime minister should resign as  a matter of principle  do you agree with that well the prime  minister is very clear  that he's covered the cost of these  refurbishments that he's been working  with officials  that he's declared in line with the  appropriate rules  but i'm not going to answer  hypotheticals about what might happen at  some  future date what the prime minister has  made clear is he has covered the costs  i think the british public understand  that they want us to get on with the job  which is delivering the vaccine program  and making sure we recover  uh our economy after covid and that we  have the jobs and growth we need across  the uk yeah but but even if we were  supposed to question  in the way of saying if any prime  minister of any  that's an even more hypothetical  question i mean that's an even more hype  but there is but there is a general rule  is that if you know if someone breaks a  ministerial code they're expected to go  and i know that ultimate decision is  made  by the prime minister but you know the  prime minister has been  very clear that he has covered the costs  of these refurbishments  if you speak to voters across the  country which i've been doing during  these elections  they fully understand they think that's  completely reasonable  okay look can we talk holidays because  i'm sure you're desperate for a holiday  like we all are do you think we are  going to be able to get away from from  may the 17th  we won't have much longer to wait uh to  get the full announcement from the  travel task force i would suggest that  people wait for  that announcement of course we're doing  all we can to make sure that  we follow our roadmap that we uh open up  the  economy and travel gradually  but the really important thing is that  we don't move  too fast and jeopardize the progress  we've made  people will have to wait a bit longer  i'm afraid to be able to hear the news  on exactly what's happening on the  travel front but  i can assure you the government's  working very hard we say  bring it in gradually is one thing you  know there's talk of this big bang  reopening thomas cook have said their  bookings are 50  higher this week than last i mean people  are chomping at the bit  of course i fully understand it's been a  very very tough year for people  uh people haven't had the opportunity to  travel either within the uk  or overseas for some time people are  looking to  book a holiday but i would encourage  people to wait until we make  that announcement so that we can see  exactly  what the details are based on the data  because what we don't want to be doing  is reimporting this virus after we've  done such an excellent job  in getting the levels down in the united  kingdom so  of course we fully support safe travel  we want to make sure that people have  the opportunity  but we need to be cautious and we need  to make sure  that we're not simply importing the  virus after we've successfully dealt  with it in britain  but to paraphrase leila moran who's  the head of the all party parliamentary  group on  coronavirus she said it is  crazy to look at reopening airports she  said airports  could be a breeding ground for the virus  she's very concerned  that says the group is very concerned  about bringing new variants back in  they want to see this all delayed at  this stage  well we are doing this in a very safe  way  so we've got a very strong testing  procedure in place  for travel we have a strong quarantine  regime and any further any opening up  of travel will be made fully safe and of  course that is why we're taking our time  the transport secretary is working very  closely with  airports to make sure that these  procedures work  but i think it's absolutely right that  once we are able to we do open these  corridors up because first of all it's  important for trade  uh it's important for economic  opportunities it's important for jobs in  graveyard britain  it's important for people with family  connections around the world to be able  to  visit and and see their families  and of course if people are if we are  able to  uh have people go on holiday i don't see  any reason they shouldn't provided it  safe  but that is the key uh question that the  task force is determining  is it safe which countries is it safe  with and we will make announcements in  due course  but i didn't cut i don't think it will  be too much longer no  before we make those announcements so i  would encourage people to  uh wait for those latest announcements  you see that there is a  sort of pressing issue here isn't there  we're talking on  you know potentially the 17th of may we  haven't had the traffic light system  announced yet we're expecting that on  the first so we don't know which  countries we can go to and come back  without quarantining  we don't know quite what's happening  with the nhs app and the  the covid passports or test  certification here i mean there's a lot  to cram in in a very short space of time  are you on track to do that we are and i  know my colleagues across government are  working very hard to make sure that  we've got those procedures in place and  now as has been said it we need to make  sure that we  do have the setup correct at the airport  so we do have  the mechanisms right before we go ahead  and make that announcement so  people are understandably you know  frustrated  you know it's been a long year uh that  we've faced this covered crisis but i  think this approach  of taking things gradually of making  sure that they're irreversible  has been the right approach and people  can have confidence  that when we make the announcement we'll  have everything in place

